WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.534
-It is great to see you, bud. Come on. You look great.

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:05.000
There's something I want to get into.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.367
but I -- I just heard this,

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:10.200
that you took your youngest -- finally saw "Hamilton."

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.901
-Yes. I took my 4-year-old, Frankie, to "Hamilton."

00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:16.734
-Little Frankie. -Little Frankie.

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:18.467
-He is the -- -Sunday matinee.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:21.267
-He is the cutest kid on Earth. I mean, he really is.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:23.100
-Yeah. -I love that kid so much.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.501
But what did he think?

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:27.467
-It was -- it was very surreal. I had the both of them.

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:29.901
and he kind of alternated between the seat next to me

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.801
and -- and my lap.

00:00:31.801 --> 00:00:34.834
"Ten Duel Commandments. Smash hit in our house.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:39.033
'Cause it's kind of like the Number-Bots song in the show.

00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:41.534
So they count in it, and he's very good at counting.

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:43.033
So he was like, "Number three."

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:44.901
He was holding his hand up like...

00:00:44.901 --> 00:00:46.501
-He's -- He's this big. -"Number four!"

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:47.934
-He's so cute.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:52.801
-And then the real surprise was "Dear Theodosia."

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:56.267
So, I didn't know this, but my wife sang him

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:59.300
"Dear Theodosia" to sleep, like, every night.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.634
[ Audience awws ] And all he asks for before --

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:03.667
We do, like, the same routine every night.

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:06.601
it's like, we read three books and listen to the Regina Spektor

00:01:06.601 --> 00:01:08.968
cover of "Dear Theodosia" from the "Mixtape."

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:11.234
So, he's only ever heard, like,

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:13.434
beautiful women singing this song.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:15.667
And they start singing it on stage.

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:17.667
and he was sitting in my lap, and I hear this little voice go,

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:20.067
"What the heck?!"

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.400
That's from this? That's from this?"

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.601
-"That's from this?" -And then --

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:28.133
"That's from this?" -"That's from this?"

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:30.200
-And then he goes -- And then he fell asleep.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.567
[ Laughter ] Because that's his bedtime song.

00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:33.968
-That's his song.

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:35.868
-He was immediately triggered to sleep.

00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:37.300
-And you're like, "You can't fall asleep during Daddy's..."

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.734
-"That's from this?"

00:01:38.734 --> 00:01:40.400
-Yeah, you can't fall asleep during Daddy's play! Wake up!

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.934
-And he just went down. He was fine by intermission.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.934
He enjoyed the second half. -Yeah. I love that kid, man.

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:45.868
-Yeah, he's the best. -Both of your kids are awesome.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:47.767
This -- I'm almost freaking out to hold this.

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:49.300
Have you held it? have you held it?

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.834
-I have held it. I have beheld it.

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:53.133
-You have? You have your own song book.

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:55.534
-Yeah. This is my -- -Dude.

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:58.868
-Collective songs. -Wait. This is --

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.400
-It's everything from

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.167
"In the Heights" through "Encanto,"

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:03.868
and it doesn't -- You know, it's kind of like --

00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:05.901
You do your show every night and you look back and you go,

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:09.067
"Oh, my God. I have done all of these shows."

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.901
To have a career's worth of songs

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:13.200
that are worthy of getting --

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.767
-Dude, just -- With the "Hal Leonard."

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.501
-The "Hal Leonard" really makes it real.

00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:18.434
-Dude, I almost got emotional because I go --

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:19.734
-Because it connects you to,

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:21.467
like, when you were learning how to play music, right?

00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:23.567
-You bought -- This was a giant thing.

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:26.534
-Yeah. I remember my books -- Like, my sheet-music books.

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:28.701
It was the complete Beatles. It was two parts.

00:02:28.701 --> 00:02:30.100
-Yeah, I had that, too. -You had --

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.367
-The Beatles fake one.

00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:32.701
-Oh, you had the Beatles fake book.

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:34.834
-I had the "Ultimate Broadway" fake book.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.167
-Wow! -And it just had, like,

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:37.534
hundreds of show tunes.

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:39.334
And a fake book is just where they have the chords

00:02:39.334 --> 00:02:40.801
over the melody.

00:02:40.801 --> 00:02:42.334
-Like, the easiest way to do the song.

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.367
-Yeah, just read the chords and kind of fake it.

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:46.367
-But it's the best. -It's the best, man.

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:48.767
-I had that. Yeah, I had that. I think I had Billy Joel.

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:52.000
-Yeah. Billy Joel. If you had a piano, you had Billy Joel.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.601
-Yeah, I couldn't do any of it. -Yeah, no.

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:55.868
-It was too advanced. I go, "Where's the fake one?"

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:57.868
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah. And what else did I --

00:02:57.868 --> 00:02:59.434
I had the "Weird Al" song book, too.

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:01.334
Of course, I had the "Weird Al" song book.

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:02.834
-You had the "Weird Al" song book?

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:04.868
-Yeah, yeah, when he released the greatest hits,

00:03:04.868 --> 00:03:06.367
it came out with the sheet music.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:07.567
-No! -Hal Leonard.

00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:09.000
-Dude. -Yeah.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.501
-Oh, my God. Not saying anything, but Christmas.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:12.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:15.200
I -- I don't have it. That'd be amazing.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.434
-Yeah.

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:18.501
-Are you excited for Al's movie? -Yes.

00:03:18.501 --> 00:03:20.334
Yeah, Weird Al's movie coming out.

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:21.667
-It's coming out. The Weird Al Yan--

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:24.234
"Weird." It's called "Weird." -It's called "Weird."

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:26.334
-Weird Al Yankovic. I cannot wait for this thing.

00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:28.100
-Yeah, Daniel Radcliffe plays Weird Al.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.300
I'm, like, plugging someone --

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.868
I have nothing to do with this movie.

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:31.934
-No. Yeah, yeah.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:32.934
-I just think you should all see it.

00:03:32.934 --> 00:03:34.234
-Yeah, exactly. Yeah. -Yeah.

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:36.334
But Weird -- Yeah, Daniel Radcliffe plays Weird Al.

00:03:36.334 --> 00:03:38.167
-Don't you have a cameo in it or something?

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:40.634
-I might have a cameo in it. -Yeah, you might have something.

00:03:40.634 --> 00:03:43.300
But when did you -- -I might have a cameo in it.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.767
-Yeah. When did you -- When did you meet Al?

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:49.367
-So, this is, like, the worst story.

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:50.834
-Oh, no! -Because I'm --

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.734
I've -- Because of "Hamilton," I met everyone.

00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:54.534
I met everyone on the Internet. I met them.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:57.434
They all came to the show. -Everyone...has been there.

00:03:57.434 --> 00:04:00.434
-Yeah, everyone you've ever googled came to "Hamilton."

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.934
But this was way -- Years pre-"Hamilton,"

00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:05.467
he was looking to maybe write a musical,

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:07.734
and he was meeting with writers.

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.634
And so I took a meeting

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:11.334
at his house... -What?

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:15.434
-...in Hollywood, and I drove there myself in a rented car.

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:17.567
And it's like meeting Santa Claus

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:18.701
meeting Weird Al.

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:20.067
-Yes! it's Weird Al Yankovic.

00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:23.667
-And I was so nervous that I, like --

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:26.133
I, like, self-destructed. Like, midway through it, I said,

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:29.834
"You know who you should work with? Bobby Lopez."

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.868
-Bobby Lopez. -Bobby Lopez, who wrote

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:33.701
"Frozen" and "Encanto" and "Book of Mormon."

00:04:33.701 --> 00:04:35.934
I was like, "He's writing a show with the 'South Park' guys.

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:38.801
They're funny. You're funny. You should write with them..."

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:41.534
and, like, talked him out of working with me.

00:04:41.534 --> 00:04:43.033
And then, like, got --

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:45.234
Some self-esteem thing happened to me.

00:04:45.234 --> 00:04:46.734
it's never happened before!

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.300
And -- And I got in my car, and I was like

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.567
the guy in "Boogie Nights."

00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:52.534
I was like, "You're an idiot. You're an idiot.

00:04:52.534 --> 00:04:54.667
Why did you do that?! Why did you do that?!"

00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:55.968
-Oh, my God. [ Sad tuba plays ]

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:57.400
-And -- Yeah.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.334
[ Imitates "Price is Right" loser horn ]

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:02.801
-Because we've talked about how much we idolized Weird Al.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:05.567
I mean, he's one of my -- -But the first time,

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:07.601
I really pooped the bed.

00:05:07.601 --> 00:05:09.300
It's amazing he's friends with me.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.400
Because the first time I was like, "H-H-Hi."

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.601
-But then you -- you ended up working together

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:15.834
when he did the "Hamilton"... -Yes, yes. Now we're...

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:17.934
-Did you write song parodies when you were a kid?

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:20.000
-I did. -Like, "Weird Al" versions?

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.334
-I dreamed -- My ambition was not as high

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:23.901
as writing musicals when I was a kid.

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:26.367
My dream was maybe Weird Al might retire

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:28.667
and I could be the next one. -Yes!

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:30.968
-That was, like, my childhood dream.

00:05:30.968 --> 00:05:32.400
-Yeah. -And --

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.934
-Of course. -I got --

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:38.033
I got straight C's in mathematics in high school,

00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:40.267
like, really C's and D's in math

00:05:40.267 --> 00:05:41.834
except for one year where --

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:45.334
God bless Miss Yallos, who was my teacher.

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:48.767
She allowed you to do creative projects to bring up your grade.

00:05:48.767 --> 00:05:51.767
And I rewrote Billy Joel's "For the Longest Time"

00:05:51.767 --> 00:05:54.434
to "Before the Law of Sines."

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:56.667
-And I explained the sine law --

00:05:56.667 --> 00:06:00.701
sine/cosine law with the lyrics, the rewritten lyrics.

00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:02.200
♪ For the law of sines ♪

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.300
♪ If you take the thing of angle B ♪

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.767
I don't remember. Again, I got C's in math.

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.868
-I think -- I think I wrote -- My first one I wrote was --

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.300
Because I was trying to be Weird Al.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.868
But I think I wrote, "I wish I Were King Kong Bundy."

00:06:15.868 --> 00:06:17.701
You know "Manic Monday"?

00:06:17.701 --> 00:06:19.133
-Oh, yeah. -And I was like --

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:21.701
♪ I wish I was King Kong Bundy ♪

00:06:21.701 --> 00:06:23.501
-♪ Whoa, oh, oh ♪

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:25.400
-♪ I wrestle on Sunday ♪

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.033
-♪ Whoa, oh, oh ♪

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:28.767
That's great! -Something like that.

00:06:28.767 --> 00:06:30.901
'Cause I was into wrestling, and I was like --

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:34.000
Yeah, King Kong Bundy, man. Yeah. Never went anywhere.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.267
-And now we're here.

00:06:36.267 --> 00:06:38.567
-Yeah, exactly. That's so wild, buddy.

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:41.234
I know you're going -- You're always doing something cool.

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:42.868
But you're off to Vegas...

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:44.100
-Yes. -...at the Venetian.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.834
Can you explain what you're doing there?

00:06:45.834 --> 00:06:48.067
-So, Freestyle Love Supreme, who has performed on the show.

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:49.367
-My fave.

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:51.334
-We are doing, like, a sitdown in Vegas.

00:06:51.334 --> 00:06:52.634
We're like Celine Dion.

00:06:52.634 --> 00:06:54.167
We're going to Vegas. We're sitting down.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:55.234
-You have a residency there?

00:06:55.234 --> 00:06:57.934
-Yeah, we have a residency at the --

00:06:57.934 --> 00:07:00.734
at the Venetian Resort, Las Vegas.

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:03.667
And we start performances November 10th.

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:07.267
But I'm jumping in the show the 16th and 17th of November.

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:08.868
And it's -- -It's the most fun show.

00:07:08.868 --> 00:07:10.501
If you've not seen it, it is --

00:07:10.501 --> 00:07:12.767
-Well, it's a completely improvised show.

00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:14.634
Like, we -- the show is based entirely

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.734
on the audience's suggestions.

00:07:16.734 --> 00:07:18.601
And one of the games we play is "Day in the Life,"

00:07:18.601 --> 00:07:20.834
where we bring someone up, we ask them about their day.

00:07:20.834 --> 00:07:24.834
Like, if you were in Vegas, you had an interesting day.

00:07:24.834 --> 00:07:27.267
Something went -- It just feels like --

00:07:27.267 --> 00:07:29.267
-Vegas is going to be insane. -Vegas is going to be insane.

00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:30.467
-You're like, "what did you do?"

00:07:30.467 --> 00:07:31.834
"Oh, get ready for what we did today."

00:07:31.834 --> 00:07:33.300
It's like, "Oh, my gosh. You did that?"

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.234
I don't even know if you can talk about what you did today."

00:07:35.234 --> 00:07:36.968
-Yeah, so I'm very excited for the chaotic energy.

00:07:36.968 --> 00:07:38.801
-Oh. Congrats on that. -Yeah.

00:07:38.801 --> 00:07:42.467
-And then "Encanto," this giant thing at the Hollywood Bowl.

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:44.067
-Yeah. -This is wild.

00:07:44.067 --> 00:07:47.334
-So, this is the voice -- the actual voice cast of "Encanto."

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.133
Steph Beatriz. Everyone you love.

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:51.133
And it's the first time performing live

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:53.100
in front of an audience.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.100
And they're singing all the songs.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:59.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:59.033 --> 00:08:02.067
-Are you going to be there? -I'm going to be there.

00:08:02.067 --> 00:08:04.901
I'm going to introduce everyone and -- and then enjoy the show.

00:08:04.901 --> 00:08:07.200
-I should say also if you can't make it to the Hollywood Bowl,

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.934
Disney Plus is going to air it on December --

00:08:08.934 --> 00:08:11.000
-Yeah, it's going to be on Disney Plus after Christmas.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.267
-December 28th. -Yeah.

00:08:12.267 --> 00:08:14.501
-Yeah. Congrats, by the way, on "Encanto" winning

00:08:14.501 --> 00:08:16.033
Best Animated Film at the Oscars.

00:08:16.033 --> 00:08:17.834
-Yeah, how about that? -Not too shabby, dude.

00:08:17.834 --> 00:08:19.334
-It's pretty crazy. -Not too shabby.

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:21.968
-Not too shabby. -Yeah.

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:25.100
Speaking of "Encanto," we're going to do something fun.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.567
Always something fun,

00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:28.400
always something musical when you come on.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.634
I want to know if you want to continue that tradition.

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:32.367
-Yeah. -Okay, good.

00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:34.100
More with Lin-Manuel Miranda when we come back everybody.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:35.300
Stick around. Come back.

